 ### [Optimization and Parallelization](6233-optimization-and-parallelization.md)
-This family encompasses the algorithmic strategies and distributed systems required to train 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) models efficiently. It solves the computational bottlenecks of differentiable rasterization and the memory limits of single-GPU training when handling billions of primitives. It is distinguished from sibling methods by its focus on system-level parallelization (multi-GPU partitioning, warp-level backprop optimization) and high-level training pipeline orchestration, rather than parameter-specific learning rate schedules or fused kernel implementations.
+This family encompasses methods that accelerate the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by optimizing the computational efficiency of the backward pass, managing memory across multiple devices, and handling specialized gradient flows such as those for probabilistic masks. It solves the bottleneck where the $O(N \cdot P)$ complexity of differentiable rasterization (where $N$ is the number of Gaussians and $P$ is the number of pixels) limits scalability to large scenes or complex representations. It is distinguished from standard 3DGS training [3761-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md] by explicitly restructuring data flow (per-splat vs. per-pixel), distributing state across GPU clusters [7349-grendel-gs.md], or computing gradients for auxiliary latent variables like masks [4254-maskgaussian-gradient-computation.md].
 
-### [CLM](7351-clm.md)
-CLM is a training optimization method for 3D Gaussian Splatting that eliminates the performance bottleneck of frequent GPU memory barriers by dynamically migrating Gaussian states between CPU and GPU memory. It solves the problem of VRAM exhaustion and transfer latency during optimization by employing a smart caching policy that retains only the most frequently updated Gaussians on the GPU. Unlike [FastGS](9177-fastgs.md) which focuses on kernel fusion, or [Fused Backward and Optimizer](9597-fused-backward-and-optimizer.md) which optimizes the compute graph, CLM optimizes data placement.
+### CLM
+CLM is a training optimization for 3D Gaussian Splatting that removes the GPU memory barrier by offloading Gaussian states to CPU memory and overlapping CPU-side optimizer updates with GPU rendering [7351-clm.md]. It solves the problem of VRAM exhaustion and communication stalls in large-scale scenes by implementing a smart caching policy that keeps frequently updated Gaussians on the GPU while streaming others [7351-clm.md]. Unlike baseline offloading which incurs significant latency, CLM uses pipelining and early Adam updates to hide communication overhead, enabling training of scenes that exceed GPU memory on a single device [7351-clm.md].
 
-### [FastGS](9177-fastgs.md)
-FastGS is a general acceleration framework for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that significantly reduces training time by regulating the number of Gaussians based on multi-view consistency, dispensing with explicit budgeting mechanisms. It re-engineers the training loop using dynamic sparse rasterization and fast gradient checkpointing to skip negligible computations. Unlike sibling methods that rely on Gaussian-associated properties (Taming-3DGS) or indirect gradient approximations (Speedy-Splat), FastGS directly evaluates each Gaussian's contribution to multi-view reconstruction quality to guide densification and pruning.
