 ## Background
-The optimization of 3D Gaussian Splatting training has evolved from the original implementation's reliance on uniform learning rates and full VRAM caching to more sophisticated strategies addressing memory constraints, convergence stability, and computational efficiency. The original 3DGS paper established the baseline differentiable rasterization and densification pipeline [S2]. Subsequent research identified specific inefficiencies, such as the oscillation in scaling parameters addressed by Faster-GS [S1, unofficial], and the memory bottlenecks of storing large datasets in VRAM, leading to disk-based loading strategies [S3, unofficial]. The need for faster training on sparse data led to reduced iteration counts [S2], while system-level optimizations like CLM and Fused Backward approaches targeted the overhead of memory transfers and optimizer kernel launches [S1, unofficial].
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1, unofficial]. The initial implementation required significant VRAM for large scenes and suffered from slow convergence due to uniform learning rates and redundant Gaussian computations. Subsequent research focused on optimizing the training loop through parameter-specific learning rates [4276-3d-gaussian-splatting-training-parameter-specific-learning-r.md], memory management via CPU offloading [7351-clm.md], and algorithmic accelerations like FastGS [9177-fastgs.md] and fused kernels [9597-fused-backward-and-optimizer.md]. These methods have become standard in large-scale reconstruction and dynamic scene modeling [S2][S3, unofficial].
 
